{"task_id": "HumanEval/80", "solution": "```python\ndef is_happy(s):\n    \"\"\"You are given a string s.\n    Your task is to check if the string is happy or not.\n    A string is happy if its length is at least 3 and every 3 consecutive letters are distinct\n    For example:\n    is_happy(a) => False\n    is_happy(aa) => False\n    is_happy(abcd) => True\n    is_happy(aabb) => False\n    is_happy(adb) => True\n    is_happy(xyy) => False\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(s) < 3:\n        return False\n\n    for i in range(len(s) - 2):\n        if s[i] == s[i+1] or s[i] == s[i+2] or s[i+1] == s[i+2]:\n            return False\n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/81", "solution": "```python\ndef numerical_letter_grade(grades):\n    \"\"\"Converts a list of numerical GPAs into a list of corresponding letter grades based on a predefined scale.\n    The function iterates through the scale from highest to lowest threshold to find the correct grade.\"\"\"\n    GRADING_SCALE = [\n        (4.0, 'A+'),\n        (3.7, 'A'),\n        (3.3, 'A-'),\n        (3.0, 'B+'),\n        (2.7, 'B'),\n        (2.3, 'B-'),\n        (2.0, 'C+'),\n        (1.7, 'C'),\n        (1.3, 'C-'),\n        (1.0, 'D+'),\n        (0.7, 'D'),\n        (0.0, 'D-'),\n        (-0.1, 'E') # Sentinel value for anything below 0.0, effectively 0.0\n    ]\n\n    result_grades = []\n    for gpa in grades:\n        for threshold, letter_grade in GRADING_SCALE:\n            if gpa >= threshold:\n                result_grades.append(letter_grade)\n                break\n    return result_grades\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/82", "solution": "```python\ndef prime_length(string):\n    \"\"\"Checks if the length of a string is a prime number.\"\"\"\n    string_length = len(string)\n\n    if string_length < 2:\n        return False\n\n    # Check for divisibility from 2 up to the square root of string_length\n    for i in range(2, int(string_length**0.5) + 1):\n        if string_length % i == 0:\n            return False\n\n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/83", "solution": "```python\ndef starts_one_ends(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive integer n, return the count of the numbers of n-digit\n    positive integers that start or end with 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int) or n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be a positive integer.\")\n\n    if n == 1:\n        return 1\n    else:\n        # Count of n-digit numbers starting with 1.\n        # The first digit is 1, and the remaining n-1 digits can be any of 0-9.\n        count_starts_with_one = 10**(n - 1)\n\n        # Count of n-digit numbers ending with 1.\n        # The last digit is 1. The first digit can be any of 1-9 (9 choices).\n        # The middle n-2 digits can be any of 0-9 (10 choices each).\n        count_ends_with_one = 9 * (10**(n - 2))\n\n        # Count of n-digit numbers starting and ending with 1.\n        # The first digit is 1, the last digit is 1.\n        # The middle n-2 digits can be any of 0-9.\n        count_starts_and_ends_with_one = 10**(n - 2)\n\n        # Apply Inclusion-Exclusion Principle:\n        # P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A and B)\n        # Here, A = starts with 1, B = ends with 1.\n        return count_starts_with_one + count_ends_with_one - count_starts_and_ends_with_one\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/84", "solution": "```python\ndef solve(N):\n    \"\"\"Given a positive integer N, return the total sum of its digits in binary.\n\n    Example\n        For N = 1000, the sum of digits will be 1 the output should be \"1\".\n        For N = 150, the sum of digits will be 6 the output should be \"110\".\n        For N = 147, the sum of digits will be 12 the output should be \"1100\".\n\n    Variables:\n        @N integer\n             Constraints: 0 \u2264 N \u2264 10000.\n    Output:\n         a string of binary number\n    \"\"\"\n\n    digit_sum = 0\n    s_N = str(N)\n    for digit_char in s_N:\n        digit_sum += int(digit_char)\n\n    if digit_sum == 0:\n        return \"0\"\n    return bin(digit_sum)[2:]\n```"}
