{"task_id": "HumanEval/132", "solution": "```python\ndef is_nested(string):\n    '''\n    Create a function that takes a string as input which contains only square brackets.\n    The function should return True if and only if there is a valid subsequence of brackets\n    where at least one bracket in the subsequence is nested.\n    '''\n    first_open_open_idx = string.find('[[')\n    if first_open_open_idx == -1:\n        return False\n\n    # Search for ']]' starting after the '[[` to ensure nesting.\n    # The search for ']]' must begin at least from the index of the second '[' + 1,\n    # which is effectively `first_open_open_idx + 2`.\n    first_close_close_idx = string.find(']]', first_open_open_idx + 2)\n\n    return first_close_close_idx != -1\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/129", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Tuple\n\ndef minPath(grid, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a grid with N rows and N columns (N >= 2) and a positive integer k,\n    each cell of the grid contains a value. Every integer in the range [1, N * N]\n    inclusive appears exactly once on the cells of the grid.\n\n    You have to find the minimum path of length k in the grid. You can start\n    from any cell, and in each step you can move to any of the neighbor cells,\n    in other words, you can go to cells which share an edge with you current\n    cell.\n    Please note that a path of length k means visiting exactly k cells (not\n    necessarily distinct).\n    You CANNOT go off the grid.\n    A path A (of length k) is considered less than a path B (of length k) if\n    after making the ordered lists of the values on the cells that A and B go\n    through (let's call them lst_A and lst_B), lst_A is lexicographically less\n    than lst_B, in other words, there exist an integer index i (1 <= i <= k)\n    such that lst_A[i] < lst_B[i] and for any j (1 <= j < i) we have\n    lst_A[j] = lst_B[j].\n    It is guaranteed that the answer is unique.\n    Return an ordered list of the values on the cells that the minimum path go through.\n\n    Examples:\n\n        Input: grid = [ [1,2,3], [4,5,6], [7,8,9]], k = 3\n        Output: [1, 2, 1]\n\n        Input: grid = [ [5,9,3], [4,1,6], [7,8,2]], k = 1\n        Output: [1]\n    \"\"\"\n    class Grid:\n        def __init__(self, grid_data: List[List[int]]):\n            self._grid_data = grid_data\n            self._N = len(grid_data)\n\n        def get_value(self, row: int, col: int) -> int:\n            return self._grid_data[row][col]\n\n        def is_valid(self, row: int, col: int) -> bool:\n            return 0 <= row < self._N and 0 <= col < self._N\n\n        def get_neighbors(self, row: int, col: int) -> List[Tuple[int, int]]:\n            neighbors = []\n            directions = [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)]  # Up, Down, Left, Right\n            for dr, dc in directions:\n                n_row, n_col = row + dr, col + dc\n                if self.is_valid(n_row, n_col):\\\n                    neighbors.append((n_row, n_col))\n            return neighbors\n\n        def get_N(self) -> int:\n            return self._N\n\n    class Path:\n        def __init__(self, initial_value: int):\n            self._values = [initial_value]\n            self._length = 1\n\n        def append(self, cell_value: int) -> None:\n            self._values.append(cell_value)\n            self._length += 1\n\n        def get_values(self) -> List[int]:\n            return list(self._values)  # Return a copy to prevent external modification\n\n        def get_length(self) -> int:\n            return self._length\n\n        def is_lexicographically_smaller(self, other_path_values: List[int]) -> bool:\n            # Assumes paths are of equal length for comparison.\\\n            return self._values < other_path_values\n\n        def copy(self) -> 'Path':\n            new_path = Path(self._values[0])  # Initialize with first value\n            new_path._values = list(self._values)  # Deep copy the list\n            new_path._length = self._length\n            return new_path\n\n    class MinPathFinder:\n        def __init__(self, grid_model: Grid, k: int):\\\n            self._grid_model = grid_model\n            self._k = k\n            # Initialize _min_path_values with a path of 'largest possible' values.\n            # Max value in grid is N*N. Use N*N + 1 to ensure any valid path is smaller.\n            max_grid_value = self._grid_model.get_N() * self._grid_model.get_N()\n            self._min_path_values = [max_grid_value + 1] * k\n\n        def find_min_path(self) -> List[int]:\n            N = self._grid_model.get_N()\n            for r in range(N):\n                for c in range(N):\n                    initial_value = self._grid_model.get_value(r, c)\n                    current_path = Path(initial_value)\n                    self._dfs(r, c, current_path)\n            return self._min_path_values\n\n        def _dfs(self, current_row: int, current_col: int, current_path: Path) -> None:\n            # Base case: Path reached target length k.\n            if current_path.get_length() == self._k:\n                if current_path.is_lexicographically_smaller(self._min_path_values):\n                    self._min_path_values = current_path.get_values()\n                return\n\n            # Pruning: Path already exceeded target length (should not happen with correct logic flow).\n            if current_path.get_length() > self._k:\n                return\n\n            # Pruning optimization: If current_path's prefix is already lexicographically greater\n            # than the current minimum path's prefix, no need to continue this branch.\n            if current_path.get_length() > 0 and current_path.get_values()[:current_path.get_length()] > self._min_path_values[:current_path.get_length()]:\n                return\n\n            # Recursive step: Explore neighbors\n            for next_row, next_col in self._grid_model.get_neighbors(current_row, current_col):\n                next_path = current_path.copy()\n                neighbor_value = self._grid_model.get_value(next_row, next_col)\n                next_path.append(neighbor_value)\n                self._dfs(next_row, next_col, next_path)\n\n    grid_model = Grid(grid)\n    finder = MinPathFinder(grid_model, k)\n    return finder.find_min_path()\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/136", "solution": "def largest_smallest_integers(lst):\n    '''\n    Create a function that returns a tuple (a, b), where 'a' is\n    the largest of negative integers, and 'b' is the smallest\n    of positive integers in a list.\n    If there is no negative or positive integers, return them as None.\n    '''\n    largest_negative = None\n    smallest_positive = None\n\n    for num in lst:\n        if num < 0:\n            if largest_negative is None or num > largest_negative:\n                largest_negative = num\n        elif num > 0:\n            if smallest_positive is None or num < smallest_positive:\n                smallest_positive = num\n\n    return (largest_negative, smallest_positive)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/130", "solution": "```python\ndef tri(n):\n    \"\"\"Calculates the first n + 1 numbers of the custom Tribonacci sequence.\n    It uses memoization to optimize recursive calculations.\"\"\"\n\n    class InvalidInputError(ValueError):\n        \"\"\"Custom exception for invalid input values.\"\"\"\n        pass\n\n    class MemoizationCache:\n        \"\"\"Provides an in-memory key-value store for memoization.\"\"\"\n        def __init__(self):\n            self._data = {}\n\n        def get(self, key: int) -> int | None:\n            return self._data.get(key)\n\n        def set(self, key: int, value: int) -> None:\n            self._data[key] = value\n\n        def clear(self) -> None:\n            self._data.clear()\n\n    class TribonacciService:\n        \"\"\"Orchestrates calculation of Tribonacci numbers and aggregates them.\"\"\"\n        def __init__(self, cache: MemoizationCache):\n            self._cache = cache\n\n        def calculate_sequence(self, n_val: int) -> list[int]:\n            \"\"\"Generates a list of the first n_val + 1 Tribonacci numbers.\"\"\"\n            if n_val < 0:\n                raise InvalidInputError(\"Input 'n' must be a non-negative integer.\")\n\n            self._cache.clear() # Clear cache for a fresh calculation\n\n            result = []\n            for k in range(n_val + 1):\n                result.append(self._get_tri_value(k))\n\n            return result\n\n        def _get_tri_value(self, k: int) -> int:\n            \"\"\"Retrieves the tri(k) value, checking cache first then calculating.\"\"\"\n            cached_value = self._cache.get(k)\n            if cached_value is not None:\n                return cached_value\n\n            calculated_value = self._calculate_tri(k)\n            self._cache.set(k, calculated_value)\n            return calculated_value\n\n        def _calculate_tri(self, k: int) -> int:\n            \"\"\"Implements the core logic for calculating tri(k) based on recurrence rules.\"\"\"\n            if k == 0:\n                return 1 # Base case: tri(0) = 1 (derived from example)\n            if k == 1:\n                return 3 # Base case: tri(1) = 3\n\n            if k % 2 == 0: # k is even\n                return 1 + k // 2\n            else: # k is odd\n                # tri(k) = tri(k - 1) + tri(k - 2) + tri(k + 1)\n                return self._get_tri_value(k - 1) + self._get_tri_value(k - 2) + self._get_tri_value(k + 1)\n\n    # Main logic for the tri(n) function\n    cache = MemoizationCache()\n    service = TribonacciService(cache)\n    return service.calculate_sequence(n)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/135", "solution": "```python\ndef can_arrange(arr):\n    \"\"\"Returns the largest index of an element which is not greater than or\n    equal to the element immediately preceding it. If no such element\n    exists then return -1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(arr) < 2:\n        return -1\n\n    # Iterate from the second-to-last element down to the first (index 1)\n    for i in range(len(arr) - 1, 0, -1):\n        if arr[i] < arr[i-1]:\n            return i\n\n    # If the loop completes, no such element was found\n    return -1\n```"}
